  "query_preview": "..\n\nThere are no big changes to expectations on the forecast for the \noverall lake effect event late tonight through Friday. The inverted \nsurface pressure trough overhead this afternoon moves southward as \nthe sharp mid level trough moves east this evening. NE low level \nwind trailing the system carries colder air into Lower Mi, colder \nbut still on the mild side in lake effect context. This leaves the \nlarger scale thermal environment dominated by warm Lake Huron \ntemperatures, warm adjoining SE Mi land areas, and the air mass \nitself as supported by observations upstream across Ontario this \nafternoon. This is expected to extend the transition timing of rain \nto rain/snow mix to all snow that is otherwise on track for mainly \nafter midnight through sunrise. Slushy accumulation up to 2 inches \ntoward the Thumb shorelines is the outcome with greatest forecast \nconfidence. \n\nAs lake effect instability ramps up late tonight and Friday morning, \nmodel solutions still offer dominant band locations off the south \nshore of Saginaw Bay and along the Thumb shoreline of Lake Huron \ndown to about the I-69 corridor. Convective depth in model soundings \npeaks around 10 kft with high CAPE density that enables updrafts \ninduced by lake instability reaching well through the DGZ and in \nexcess of -20C. Along with a stray lightning strike, this amount of \ninstability and high moisture content produces a greater than normal \ncellular/convective character within the areas of dominant band \ncoverage defined by the mean NNE wind field, leading to some spatial \nand temporal variation on the most intense lake effect snow showers. \nThe 12/12Z NBM cycle seems to reflect some of this variation with a \nslight downward trend on max QPF, max snow accumulation, and some \nbroadening of the QPF/Snow footprint. As suggested by hi-res models \nand ensembles, a window for accumulation overachievement is still in \nplay given the chance of a colder profile caused by the high \nprecipitation intensity. The lake effect is highly convective, but \nalso highly variable, which considerably reduces predictability and \nbetter monitored for short fused forecast adjustments in later \nupdates. The best forecast message for this update carries forward an\ninch or two of slushy accumulation with a chance for localized \nhigher totals until bands shift closer to the shoreline or offshore \nduring Friday afternoon. \n\nA transition toward a NW flow configuration across Lower Mi begins \nFriday night to further the offshore lake effect trend. Additional \nactivity grazes the Thumb shoreline while thermal profiles remain \nrelatively warm and convective depth gradually diminishes after \nmidnight through Saturday morning. The best chance for additional \nlight, slushy accumulation is during the diurnal minimum Friday \nnight and Saturday morning. However, boundary layer thermal profiles \nwarm sufficiently in model soundings for an all rain precip type and \nreally don't cool back to near or below freezing before bands shift \nmore fully into southern Lake Huron Saturday afternoon.\n\nThe highly amplified mid/upper air pattern shifts slowly eastward \nSunday into early next week. Low level cold air is carried out of \nthe Great Lakes as high pressure moves overhead, deactivating the \nLakes and decreasing clouds Sunday night. Normal high temperatures \nare now in the mid to upper 40s which is in the range of advertised \nguidance high temperatures that gradually warm back above normal \ntoward the mid week period.",
